WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.960
For decades, the global technology market
operated as a unified,

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.320
borderless enterprise. The assumption was
simple.

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.000
The best product would naturally win out,
regardless of where

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.320
it was designed or manufactured.

5
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.240
That assumption is being replaced by
sovereign statecraft.

6
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.080
In official policy documents, the United
States has stated that

7
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.920
its objective is unquestioned and
unchallenged global technological dominance.

8
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.320
The White House AI Action Plan pairs
offensive domestic innovation

9
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.800
with defensive economic statecraft.

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.760
It uses semiconductor export controls to
actively degrade China's capacity

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.240
to compete while exporting full-stack AI
packages to allies to

12
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.040
create durable technological dependency.

13
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.560
This strategy is meeting two other
distinct visions.

14
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.400
China's new generation AI development plan
seeks to make the

15
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.320
country the world's primary innovation
center by 2030, while the

16
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.720
EU uses its AI Act to assert its position
as

17
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.200
a global regulatory superpower.

18
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.560
These forces are fracturing the global
digital order.

19
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.440
What was once a single tech stack is
splitting into

20
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.920
competing and increasingly incompatible
technological ecosystems.

21
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.480
The sharpest front of this fracture is at
the lowest

22
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.880
layer of the stack, the hardware.

23
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.360
In October 2022, the U.S.

24
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.440
implemented sweeping export controls
designed to sever China's access to

25
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.840
high-end semiconductor technology.

26
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.520
These controls target the manufacturing
equipment needed to produce anything

27
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.520
below 14 nanometers.

28
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.520
It is a decisive shift from denying
specific military applications

29
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.040
to impeding an entire sector of a rival's
economy.

30
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.800
To sustain this, the U.S.

31
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.160
formed the CHIP4 alliance, linking
American design, Dutch lithography, Japanese

32
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.880
materials, and Asian manufacturing.

33
00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.360
But the alliance faces internal friction.

34
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.760
South Korean firms depend on the Chinese
market, caught between

35
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.040
their security ally and their largest
commercial buyer.

36
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.560
These restrictions have also accelerated
Chinese self-reliance.

37
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.600
Facing crippling sanctions, Huawei
developed a 7-nanometer chip using existing

38
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.880
technology, while models like DeepSeek V3
demonstrated frontier-level performance at

39
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.440
a fraction of the traditional training
cost.

40
00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.640
The result is that pursuing independence
from a rival has

41
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.960
forced deeper interdependence with allies,
while restrictions on hardware have

42
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.560
spurred the very domestic breakthroughs
they were intended to prevent.

43
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.840
Moving up the stack to the infrastructure
layer, cloud data

44
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.960
centers are no longer just logistical
backrooms.

45
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.120
The U.S. dominates global compute through
AWS, Azure, and Google

46
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.840
Cloud, and it is beginning to treat cloud
access as

47
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.520
a tool of statecraft.

48
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.040
This dominance has fueled data sovereignty
movements worldwide.

49
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.120
The U.S. Cloud Act gives American law
enforcement access to

50
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.240
data held by U.S.

51
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.640
firms, regardless of its physical
location, intensifying concerns over data

52
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.320
localization. This has created a market
for what amounts to

53
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.480
sovereignty as a service, cloud providers
that sell the feeling

54
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.320
of sovereign control while deepening
actual dependency on foreign-owned infrastructure.

55
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.200
At the software layer, there is an
internal clash over

56
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.560
open source AI.

57
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.800
Meta argues releasing models ensures the
American ecosystem remains the

58
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.880
global standard. But national security
experts counter that open sourcing

59
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:27.320
models hands competitors billions in free
RD, neutralizing semiconductor export

60
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:32.040
controls. These two pillars of American
strategy, championing a global

61
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.360
innovation ecosystem or pursuing
technological containment, cannot be done simultaneously.

62
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.240
The competition will be won or lost in the
nations

63
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.120
of the global south, which represent the
majority of the

64
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.720
world's population. China has been
systematic here, using its digital

65
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.360
Silk Road to provide affordable turnkey AI
solutions, smart city

66
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.240
technology, surveillance systems, and
digital government platforms, all bundled with

67
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.000
financing. The U.S.

68
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.840
approach has been perceived as less
coherent, focusing more on

69
00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.960
restricting access to rivals than on
offering viable technological alternatives.

70
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.200
But these nations are not passive.

71
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.600
Organizations like the African Union and
Mercosur are pooling diplomatic

72
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.000
capital to co-develop their own standards,
build regional compute hubs,

73
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.840
and negotiate as blocks to avoid digital
colonialism.

74
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.040
Strategy now requires accounting for
competing blocks, divergent standards, and

75
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.800
weaponized supply chains.

76
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.960
The fiction of a single, unified global
technology market is

77
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.560
over. For those building in the AI space,
the tools,

78
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.280
platforms, and standards we adopt are no
longer neutral technical

79
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.880
choices. They are explicit geopolitical
alignments.

80
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.880
The fracture is here.

